  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLatest surface analysis places a cold front across southern\nQuebec with a pre-frontal trough draped across the forecast\narea. This trough has allowed for mostly cloudy skies across\nmuch of the area along with areas of showers moving across\ncentral NH into SW Maine. These clouds and showers have put a\nhalt on destabilization while areas near the Canadian Border \nhas seen some filtered sunshine and MU CAPE build to around 500 \nJ/kg. Latest radar shows thunderstorms along and ahead of the\ncold front with these forecast to move into areas north of the\nmountains through early this evening. These storms will track\ntowards the Kennebec Valley through sunset. Limited CAPE will\nlikely keep storms sub-severe while some may contain gusty winds\nup to 40 mph.\n\nThe cold front will progress southward tonight with showers\nweakening as they reach the coast. Much of tonight will be dry\nwith patchy fog possible, especially in valley locations and\nareas that see some rainfall. Lows tonight with range from the\n40s north to 50s south.\n\nHigh pressure will be in control Monday for fair weather and\ntemperatures ranging from the 70s north to low 80s south. Light\nand variable winds will turn onshore along the coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA ridge builds into the Great Lakes through the week, while New\nEngland looks to remain on the eastern periphery of the ridge. \nThe ridge continues to amplify across Hudson Bay by late in the \nweek, then folds over across northern Quebec. While remaining on\nthe edge of the ridge midweek, New England looks to become \nenveloped in a deepening trough across Atlantic Canada by late \nin the week. This brings a cooler and unsettled pattern, that \nmay extend into Labor Day weekend as well.\n\nThe first system arrives in New England late Monday night and \nTuesday. Low pressure tracks through southern Canada, while a \nwarm front looks likely to stall across New England and serve to\nfocus rounds of showers and storms. The first round looks to \nmiss most of the area to the south and west, leaving southwest \nNew Hampshire the most likely spot to see showers and storms \nwith the first round.\n\nThe front drifts north a bit into central New England by late \nWednesday and Thursday. A fresh wave of moisture drifts \nnorthward into the area at the same time, while the low pressure\ncenter makes its closest pass through southern Quebec. This \nbrings the best chance for the most widespread showers and \nstorms to much of the area.\n\nBy late Thursday and Friday, the trough looks to deepen south \nof Nova Scotia. With blocking off to north, any moisture or \nshowers would have to track southeastward to exit New England. \nThis keeps the chance for showers ongoing into at least \nSaturday. The best chance for New England to dry out for Labor \nDay weekend would be for the trough to deepen more south of Nova\nScotia, resulting in a more consolidated trough and low \npressure system. There is some support for this, mainly from the\nEuro AIFS. New England would still mostly remain in a cooler \ntroughing pattern, but with a brighter and drier northwesterly \nflow pattern. Much uncertainty remains in the evolution of this \nfeature from Friday onward, but its worth noting that a wet \nweekend is far from a lock at this point.",
